<p>
The Australian government should start a theatre company.
</p>

<p>
Seriously, I'm not sure what else to compare what's going on in this country to.
Maybe a dystopian social science fiction novel?
But it doesn't necessarily seem like malice is the key driver here.
Hanlon's Razor.
</p>

<hr />

<p>
For those unaware, Australia instituted a social media ban.
I don't use social media: I have a YouTube account (which I rarely use) and one Instagram account (which I never use, and which has no identifying information on it).
So, this ban has not yet affected me; and even if it does I know how to use a VPN (which is a temporary measure, since other countries are also slowly implementing these bans).
</p>

<p>
Of course, the scope of the age verification checking is slowly expanding.
Some <a href="https://ia.acs.org.au/article/2026/search-engine-age-checking-rules-begin-in-australia.html">search engines</a> are now being targeted, though I use DuckDuckGo and am also immune there for the time being.
We don't yet have any regulation drafting age verification at the OS level, as they do in some states in the USA.
It wouldn't be a surprise if we get them soon, though.
</p>

<p>
So why am I talking about this now?
Well, a few weeks ago, eSafety published their evaluation of the ban, which you can read <a href="https://www.esafety.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-07/Early-days-early-insights-SMMA-evaluation-three-months-July-2026.pdf?v=1785826728793">here</a>.
In my opinion, this report and the response by Andrew Leigh has solidified and made readily visible the true purpose of this ban and digital age verification.
But we'll get to that.
First, what about the report?
</p>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="esafety-for-those-who-don-t-know">eSafety for those who don't know</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
The office of the eSafety commissioner (read as Big Brother) is a regulatory agency within Australia, independent of the government.
Its purpose is to regulate online safety, which originally meant dealing with cyberbullying but has now expanded to "making sure children (and you) don't see what we don't want to".
</p>

<p>
You may think that description cynical.
However, eSafety does not really have a history of any notable action until the social media ban.
It's chiefly been responsible for requesting that certain content (generally cyberbullying posts) be deleted.
The way that it's gone about doing this is by abusing its authority to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ESafety_Commissioner">get posts removed with informal notices</a>, which are then treated as formal legal requests by the platform.
In terms of formal legal action, it essentially does not do anything.
<a href="https://www.acma.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-10/ACMA%20and%20eSafety%20annual%20report%202024-25.pdf?">Four formal takedown notices in a year.</a>
A classic waste of taxpayer dollars.
</p>

<p>
The commissioner herself is Julie Inman Grant, who previously worked for both Microsoft and Adobe.
I want to keep the tinfoil hat off, let's just say neither Microsoft or Adobe are very ethical companies and leave it at that.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="key-findings-of-the-report">Key findings of the report</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
Ok, so now let's talk about the headline findings of this report.
There are 9:
</p>
<ol class="org-ol">
<li>The number of children who owned a social media account declined. (52.4% to 42.1%)</li>
<li>Children who continue to hold an account, hold one because platforms are not instituting the ban properly.</li>
<li>Children moved to different platforms as a result of the ban.
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>Specifically, platforms which should be age restricted in Reddit and Kick.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>Parental awareness of children's digital activity declined. (Parents who were unaware of activity increased from 23% to 33%)</li>
<li>Perception of social media overall became more negative.</li>
<li>Parental support for the ban remained the same.</li>
<li>Most children believed the ban had no impact on them.</li>
<li>Family dynamics did not really change.</li>
<li>There were some age and gender differences in the above.</li>
</ol>

<p>
Even if you take this at face value, it's really unimpressive.
80% of all kids still own their social media account and have seen little impact according to this report.
</p>

<p>
But, there are some extra caveats:
</p>
<ol class="org-ol">
<li>All the children asked were in the age range of 10 to 15.</li>
<li>Children <i>self-reported</i> these answers.</li>
</ol>

<p>
All the messaging around this ban has framed it as doing something wrong, or illegal.
Tell me, do you seriously believe a 10 to 15 year old would be honest if you suddenly made something they were used to illegal, and asked them if they were using it?
I would <b>certainly</b> lie!
Hell, I lie all the time on random surveys as a privacy protection measure.
It's not something to hold against theese children, I think it's a completely reasonable response!
</p>

<p>
So, accounting for dishonesty, what has this ban actually accomplished?
It hasn't substantially reduced the number of children holding a social media account.
It's impossible to tell if it really has or hasn't had an impact on the children affected.
It <b>has</b> made parents reliant on the legislation instead of maintaining their own awareness of their children's digital presence.
And it's created a framework and precedent for digital age verification in all services.
</p>

<p>
I also doubt this claim:
</p>
<blockquote>
<p>
– other attempts to bypass the restrictions, including parental assistance to do so or using a VPN, were less common. 
</p>
</blockquote>
<p>
At 14 to 15, I had begun to take my data privacy much more seriously.
I stopped using Gmail and moved to Tutanota.
I stopped using Windows and started using Linux.
I want to hope that the next generation is digitally literate enough to realise the problems with this legislation, and walk around it.
</p>

<p>
So, what the fuck was the point of this?
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="the-response">The response</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
I'm not the first person to notice all this shit, and of course the media did what the media does and <a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/australia-defends-teen-social-media-ban-after-study-shows-most-still-online-2026-08-01/">contacted the government for comment</a>.
</p>
<blockquote>
<p>
Confronted with the regulator's findings, Andrew Leigh, Assistant Minister for Productivity, Competition, Charities and Treasury, argued the ban had already reshaped the ​national debate.
</p>

<p>
The ban has been "an important game changer in the conversation among ​parents," Leigh said in televised remarks, according to an official transcript.
</p>

<p>
"We've had millions of accounts shut ‌down," ⁠Leigh said in televised remarks, according to an official transcript.
</p>

<p>
"We never expected that this would have 100% compliance. We don't get 100% compliance out of minimum drinking age laws, but it's still appropriate that ​we have that ​law on the ⁠books."
</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
So we have "millions" of accounts shut down, in a country with a population of 27 million.
One has to wonder who was affected, since the children in the report certainly weren't.
</p>

<p>
Now we're saying that the point of this ban was to "change the converstion"?
We instituted national digital surveillance infrastructure, soon rivalling China (who we mock for having a social credit system that doesn't exist), to "change the conversation"?
We made ID verification a requirement for searching Google, to "change the conversation"?
Come the fuck on.
</p>

<p>
You make legislation because you <b>expect</b> compliance.
Drinking age laws exist and yes, some kids will drink anyway, but there is no way that 80% of children continued to drink from before drinking age laws were implemented.
This is an extremely disingenuous comparison.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="why-you-should-be-against-this-ban">Why you should be against this ban</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
I realise that a lot of people don't understand the impact this will have if it continues to move forward and absorb more scope that it doesn't need.
</p>

<p>
Limiting access for children limits access for all of us.
The internet is a bastion of free information and expression.
Your browsing data is incredibly revealing about you, it's how targeted advertising works.
There's plenty of things we don't want Big Brother to know, but that can all be inferred from just the sites you visit.
</p>

<p>
Age verification is also a time bomb.
With generative AI, impersonating people is easier than ever, and it's made even easier if your government ID is available to the fraudsters.
If you comply with age verification laws, then your government ID is digitized somewhere, and the database that holds it being hacked is only a matter of time.
This isn't a theoretical, it already happened at <a href="https://proton.me/blog/discord-age-verfication-breach">Discord</a>.
</p>

<p>
Optus and Telstra get hacked every few years.
If you seriously think that keeping your ID in some database operated by some unknown age verification provider is any safer, well&#x2026;
I only hope you're keeping your money in cash, so it can't be siphoned from your bank account when your password is stolen.
</p>

<p>
You will be impacted by this.
</p>

<p>
You probably already are.
</p>

<p>
You just don't see it yet.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="survival-in-the-digital-panopticon">Survival in the digital panopticon</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
But it's not all doom.
It's actually trivial to avoid all of this crap, and you should be doing it.
The government, through your ISP, can see your web traffic already (to a limited extent).
Specifically, I suspect they're looking at the usage of tools that avoid the age verification, as an indicator for how popular it really is.
</p>

<p>
So, use those tools.
Here's a quick guide to what you can do.
</p>
</div>

<div class="outline-3">
<h3 id="vpns">VPNs</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3">
<p>
You should have a VPN.
The one you use doesn't particularly matter, but I would advise against anything that spends a good amount of money advertising via social media.
</p>

<p>
Specifically, AVOID NordVPN, Private Internet Access, and Surfshark.
</p>

<p>
Instead, use Proton VPN, or Mullvad (which is the best option for security, by a mile).
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-3">
<h3 id="operating-systems">Operating Systems</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3">
<p>
OS age verification is coming, and once it arrives there will be no escaping the surveillance state we've built.
</p>

<p>
The only way to avoid it is to use free (<a href="https://www.fsf.org/about">as in freedom</a>) and open source software which can be modified to remove any age verification.
Linux is the obvious place to start; I recommend Mint for beginners.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-3">
<h3 id="encrypted-messaging">Encrypted Messaging</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3">
<p>
Encryption is also still legal for the time being.
Make sure you use chat or messaging apps that support end to end encryption, and make sure that it's turned on.
Again, prioritise free and open source apps for the same reasons as operating systems above:
</p>

<p>
Signal and Element are the two best options in this space currently.
</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-3">
<h3 id="education">Education</h3>
<div class="outline-text-3">
<p>
If you want to understand the problems underlying this legislation, read into the efforts of the <a href="https://www.eff.org/about">EFF</a> and <a href="https://www.fsf.org/about">FSF</a>.
These are American organisations, but their philosophies are important to understand here.
</p>

<p>
Actually, the EFF has an Australian branch (<a href="https://efa.org.au/about/">EFA</a>) that was involved in the design and research of the current age verification measures.
They have been fighting this every step of the way, and raised concerns early about <a href="https://efa.org.au/preliminary-findings-of-the-australian-governments-age-assurance-technology-trial/">age verification system</a>.
They resigned from the advisory board investigating this technology, because their concerns were being ignored.
Donate to them, or at least read their articles.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="outline-2">
<h2 id="closing">Closing</h2>
<div class="outline-text-2">
<p>
The Australian government is hell-bent on beating China at the surveillance game.
Don't forget legislative gems like <a href="https://www.homeaffairs.gov.au/about-us/our-portfolios/national-security/lawful-access-telecommunications/surveillance-legislation-amendment-identify-and-disrupt-act-2021">Identify and Disrupt</a> that give insane surveillance powers to the AFP.
Palantir has as much of a presence here as in the USA.
Cops are rolling out AI surveillance similar to Flock cameras in the USA, and no one is saying a thing.
</p>

<p>
We're already in the cyberpunk dystopia.
And not the cool kind.
</p>

<p>
Like I said, I don't think that these laws are necessarily malicious by nature.
I just think the political class don't know or care about the impacts that this will have, and they're being sold a lie by consultants that stand to gain millions from these laws.
</p>

<p>
We now have evidence from the horsess mouth that shows that these efforts simply <b>are not working</b>, at the cost of the liberty of everyone in this country.
So why are we still expanding the scope?
</p>

<p>
Keep pushing this question.
</p>
</div>
